<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Network Security Configuration.

    Problem being solved:
    The app is distributed as a debug APK. Android's default network security config for
    debug builds includes user-installed CA certificates in the trust anchors:
        <debug-overrides>
            <certificates src="user" />   ← allows MITM by anyone who installs a proxy CA cert
        </debug-overrides>

    This matters because the app sends sensitive data over HTTPS:
    - Claude API calls: includes the user's API key in the x-api-key header and base64-encoded
      financial PDF content in the request body.
    - GitHub update check/download: APK served via HTTPS.

    With a user-installed proxy CA cert (possible without root via Settings → Security →
    Install certificate), an attacker with brief physical device access can intercept and read
    all of this traffic.

    Fix:
    Explicitly declare system CAs as the only trust anchor for all builds. This overrides
    the debug-build default that adds user CAs. All remote endpoints (api.anthropic.com,
    raw.githubusercontent.com, github.com) use DigiCert/GlobalSign roots that are present
    in the Android system trust store, so this does not affect functionality.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
